# Deliberately empty of rules. Two Cloudflare Pages behaviours make the obvious
# SPA rule unusable here, and both were found the hard way -- record them so
# nobody re-adds it.
#
#   /app/*  /app/index.html  200
#     Silently DISCARDED. Pages canonicalises /app/index.html back to /app/,
#     which re-matches /app/*, so it detects an infinite loop and drops the rule.
#     wrangler warns; the deployed site just serves the landing page for every
#     route inside the app. The same rule works on Netlify, which is where it
#     came from.
#
#   /app/*  /app-shell  200        (shell moved outside the pattern)
#     Parses fine and is far worse. Cloudflare evaluates _redirects BEFORE
#     static assets -- the reverse of Netlify -- so this rewrites *everything*
#     under /app/ to the shell: every script, the stylesheet, the manifest and
#     the service worker all get served as text/html and the app never boots.
#
# The SPA fallback is dist/app/404.html instead, a copy of the app's index.html
# made in CI. Pages serves the nearest 404.html walking up the tree, so it
# catches client-side routes without touching real assets. The cost is that a
# deep link returns a 404 status while serving a working app; a Pages Function
# would be needed for a true 200.
